A bind takes any share, not only one the invoked capability names.
A thread would run on a share its binder does not hold, or on one there is not;
a thread's share lies within the capability it was bound through.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: slot is not derived from its parent

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -560,9 +560,6 @@ static int op_share(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
         if (err != KERR_OK) {
             return err;
         }
-        if (arg[2] >= cap->b) {
-            return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
-        }
         /* The binding hangs below the invoked capability, so a revoke above it unbinds the thread. */
         sched_bind((struct thread *)cap_object(&tc), cap->a + arg[2], slot_node(t, slot));
         return KERR_OK;
